  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Inclusive and sustainable coffee value chain in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Building on the preparatory assistance phase (PA) funded by the UN JSDG Acceleration  Fund for Food Systems Transformation, UNIDO and FAO will be implementing a Joint Programme to unleash the potential of the coffee value chain. A comprehensive approach from farm and productivity (FAO) to value addition, entrepreneurship and skills development, informed market access, and an enabling political and financial environment (UNIDO) will be undertaken to ensure holistic transformation of the value chain. Both agencies will focus on enhancing the inclusion and participation of women, youth, and other marginalized communities Coffee in Kenya currently contributes to 30% of the labor force employed in agriculture. However, following a drastic decline in economic viability and productivity over the last several decades, labor force engagement with the sector has decreased. High input costs, intermittent income due to a short farming season, reliance on global market trends, convoluted regulations and asymmetric information access across the value chain are all factors that have stifled development in the sector. This coupled with the historic marginalization of women and youth has not helped address the high unemployment rates, particularly in rural coffee growing areas. Despite this, the coffee sector retains its potent</narrative>
  </description>
